    faqs: [
      { q: 'Is Smartious Study Abroad free for Canadian applications?', a: 'Yes — completely free for students. No consultation fees, no application processing charges, no hidden costs. All Smartious study abroad services are provided at zero cost to you or your family.' },
      { q: 'How much does it cost to study in Canada?', a: 'International tuition typically CAD 15,000-40,000/year for most undergraduate programmes (some specialised programmes like engineering or business can reach CAD 50,000-60,000). Living costs CAD 12,000-18,000/year depending on city (Toronto and Vancouver highest). Total: CAD 27,000-60,000/year. Substantially lower than US private universities and comparable to UK.' },
      { q: 'What\'s the difference between universities and colleges in Canada?', a: 'Canadian universities offer degree programmes (Bachelor\'s, Master\'s, PhD). Canadian colleges (often called community colleges or technical colleges) offer diploma and certificate programmes — typically 1-3 years, more applied/vocational, often with strong industry connections. Both qualify graduates for the Post-Graduation Work Permit. For graduate-level academic study, choose university. For applied technical careers, colleges can be excellent and often faster.' },
      { q: 'What KCSE grade do I need for Canadian universities?', a: 'For top Canadian universities (U of T, McGill, UBC), KCSE A- (Minus) and above is competitive. For most other universities, KCSE B+ (Plus) is sufficient. Some universities accept KCSE B (Plain) for mid-tier programmes. Strong subject grades in relevant areas (Mathematics for engineering/CS, Sciences for medicine) are often more important than overall mean grade.' },
      { q: 'How long is the Post-Graduation Work Permit?', a: 'PGWP length matches your study programme: 8-month programme → 8-month PGWP; 1-year programme → 1-year PGWP; 2+ year programme → 3-year PGWP (maximum). The PGWP is a one-time permit — you can\'t renew it. Most students complete a 2+ year programme specifically to qualify for the full 3-year PGWP.' },
      { q: 'Can my family come with me to Canada?', a: 'Yes — spouses and common-law partners can typically apply for an Open Work Permit (allowing work for any employer) while you study. Children can attend Canadian public schools, often without separate study permits. This makes Canada particularly attractive for mature students with families.' },
      { q: 'What\'s Express Entry and how does it work?', a: 'Express Entry is Canada\'s main system for skilled worker permanent residence. You create a profile, get scored on the Comprehensive Ranking System (CRS) based on age, education, work experience, language ability, etc. Top-scoring candidates are invited to apply for PR. Canadian-educated graduates with Canadian work experience score significantly higher than overseas applicants — making post-graduation PR very achievable.' },
      { q: 'How long until I can become a Canadian permanent resident?', a: 'Typical pathway for Kenyan students: complete 2+ year programme (2-4 years) → PGWP (1-3 years) → 12 months Canadian work experience → Express Entry CRS score 470+ → invitation to apply for PR → permanent residence granted. Total typical timeline from student arrival to PR: 4-7 years. Canadian citizenship: 3 additional years after PR.' },
      { q: 'Can I work during my studies?', a: 'Yes — international students with valid study permits can work up to 20 hours/week off-campus during academic terms and full-time during vacation breaks. On-campus work is unlimited. Typical wages CAD 14-18/hour helps with living costs.' },
      { q: 'Why is Canada more popular than the US for international students now?', a: 'Three reasons: (1) clearer post-study work and PR pathway (3-year PGWP vs US OPT uncertainty), (2) more affordable tuition, (3) more welcoming policy environment for immigrants. The US still leads for ultra-elite institutions (Ivy League) and STEM research, but Canada wins for most Kenyan students seeking quality education + long-term residence.' },
      { q: 'Do I need to do French if I study in Quebec?', a: 'No — McGill, Concordia and Bishop\'s University in Quebec teach in English. Université de Montréal, HEC Montréal and Laval teach primarily in French. If you study in English at McGill or Concordia, you can graduate without French. Knowing some French helps for daily life in Montreal and for Quebec immigration pathways, but isn\'t required academically.' },
      { q: 'What\'s WES credential evaluation?', a: 'World Education Services (WES) is the most commonly used credential evaluation service for international qualifications applying to Canada. WES converts your KCSE results into Canadian-equivalent grades, which Canadian universities use to assess your application. We help students complete the WES evaluation correctly — including which subjects to include and how to organise your documentation.' },
      { q: 'Are there scholarships for African students?', a: 'Yes — substantially. The Mastercard Foundation Scholars Programme provides full funding for African students at U of T, UBC, McGill and several other Canadian universities. The Pearson International Scholarship at U of T is one of the world\'s most generous undergraduate awards. Most Canadian universities also offer entrance scholarships of CAD 1,000-25,000 to high-performing international students, often automatically with admission.' },
    ],
