    faqs: [
      { q: 'Is studying in Germany really free?', a: 'Public university tuition is free or near-free in most German Länder (federal states) for international students. You pay only an administrative semester fee of €150-350/semester. Exception: Baden-Württemberg charges €1,500/semester for non-EU international students. Total tuition cost over a 3-year Bachelor\'s degree is typically €1,000-3,000 — vastly cheaper than UK/US/Australia.' },
      { q: 'Can I study in Germany without speaking German?', a: 'Yes — Germany now offers 1,800+ English-taught Bachelor\'s and Master\'s programmes. Many top programmes in engineering, sciences, business and computer science are available in English. However, learning German is highly recommended for daily life, social integration and post-study employment opportunities.' },
      { q: 'What is uni-assist?', a: 'uni-assist e.V. is a centralised application processing service used by about 175 German universities. You submit one application via uni-assist (with your documents), and uni-assist forwards your application to your chosen German universities. This streamlines international applications. Application fee: €75 for first university, €30 for each additional.' },
      { q: 'How much money do I need for living costs?', a: 'Plan for €10,000-12,000/year living costs. The official blocked account requirement is currently €11,208 for one year — this money is locked and released to you monthly during your studies. Living costs vary by city: Munich and Frankfurt are most expensive; Leipzig, Dresden, smaller cities are more affordable.' },
      { q: 'What\'s a blocked account and how does it work?', a: 'A blocked account (Sperrkonto) is a German bank account where you deposit your annual living cost requirement before your visa is approved. The money is "blocked" — you can only withdraw a set monthly amount (~€934/month). This proves to German authorities that you can support yourself. Major providers: Deutsche Bank, Fintiba, Expatrio.' },
      { q: 'Can I work while studying in Germany?', a: 'Yes — international students can work 120 full days or 240 half days per year. Typical part-time wages €11-15/hour. Working as a research assistant at your university (Hilfskraft) often pays better. Working more than the allowed days requires permission from the immigration authority.' },
      { q: 'What KCSE grade do I need for German universities?', a: 'For top German universities (TUM, LMU, Heidelberg, RWTH), KCSE B+ (Plus) and above with strong subject grades is competitive. For mid-tier universities, KCSE B (Plain) is sufficient. Some pre-university Studienkolleg programmes accept lower grades and prepare students for direct university entry. Strong Mathematics grades are particularly important for engineering and STEM programmes.' },
      { q: 'What is a Studienkolleg?', a: 'A Studienkolleg is a one-year preparatory programme for international students whose secondary qualifications don\'t directly qualify them for German university admission. Different focus tracks (M-Kurs for medicine/sciences, T-Kurs for technical, W-Kurs for business) prepare you for the Feststellungsprüfung — the qualification assessment exam. Studienkolleg attendance is free at public institutions.' },
      { q: 'Can my family come with me to Germany?', a: 'Yes — spouses and minor children can apply for family reunification visas. Spouses usually need basic German (A1) to qualify for the family reunification visa. Once together in Germany, spouses can typically work without restrictions. Children attend German public schools (free).' },
      { q: 'What\'s the EU Blue Card?', a: 'The EU Blue Card is Germany\'s skilled worker residence permit for graduates with university degrees earning above the threshold (€43,800/year as of 2026, or €39,624 for shortage occupations like IT, engineering, medicine). EU Blue Card holders can apply for permanent residence after 21-33 months. Citizenship is achievable after 5 years of permanent residence.' },
      { q: 'Why is Germany cheaper than other Western destinations?', a: 'Germany\'s education is publicly funded — taxpayers pay for university education as a public good, including for international students. This reflects German policy of attracting skilled workers and integrating them into the German economy long-term. The free tuition model is part of Germany\'s strategy to address skilled labour shortages.' },
      { q: 'Are German degrees recognised internationally?', a: 'Yes — German university degrees are recognised globally and respected particularly in engineering, sciences and research. The Bologna Process aligns German Bachelor\'s and Master\'s degrees with European standards. PhDs from German universities are highly regarded internationally. German engineering qualifications often have stronger reputation than their tuition cost suggests.' },
    ],
